<h1 class="page-title">Okiro</h1>
<p class="standfirst">Sound design, music &amp; implementation &middot; 2024 &middot; out now on Steam</p>

<p>OKIRO is a 3D puzzle adventure set in a world swallowed by toxic fog. You play Lulu, a little plant sprite, exploring the ruins of a factory and collecting energy stones to grow seeds that clean the air. It came out on Steam in October 2024.</p>
<p>Fellow RMIT sound designer Nick and I did all of its audio, splitting the music, sound design and implementation between us. Our idea was to make Lulu sound like the one soft, living thing in a world of machines. I wrote the music for the indoor areas, and designed and implemented much of the sound in Unreal Engine with Blueprints.</p>

{% video { youtube: "rfTi4LxGYUo", title: "Okiro audio showcase", caption: "Audio showcase: Okiro's sound and music in the game." } %}

<h2>What I did</h2>
<ul class="points">
  <li>Music: the theme for the indoor areas, in the same key as Nick's outdoor theme</li>
  <li>Sound design: Lulu's movement and abilities, boxes, vans, moving platforms, pipes, fans, bells, a ship, and plants drying out and coming back to life</li>
  <li>Implementation in Unreal Engine 4.27: Blueprints, sound cues, attenuation, concurrency and audio volumes</li>
  <li>Sound for the opening and closing cutscenes</li>
</ul>

<h2>Sound</h2>
<p>Lulu was the hardest sound to get right. The developers didn't want her to have a voice, so after trying little grunts, then my cat's meows, her jump became a band-pass filter swept over noise: cartoony, but not a voice. Her steps and landings are foley from a soft toy about her size.</p>
<p>Most of the rest I recorded around the house: a milk crate dragged across concrete for the boxes, a mechanical clock for the clocks, and finger chimes, layered, pitched and washed in modulated reverb, for the magic bells. A library recording of a snake's hiss turned out to be the most convincing burn from the hot pipes.</p>

